Feature: the scheduled end of day picks its lifecycle verb from configuration

  # PARKED — do not promote to .feature without a human revocation on record.
  #
  # This was BL-762's scenario 06. It is parked, not deleted, because the
  # design is sound and only its precondition is currently vetoed.
  #
  # Why parked (specifier, 2026-08-08):
  #
  #   1. There is no scheduled day-shift-end path to hang a verb off. Nothing
  #      in the repo or the live crontab stops the swarm on a timer; the only
  #      swarmforge cron entry is the BL-675 freshness check. The Telegram
  #      operator's `/shift end` (applyShiftEnd in
  #      extension/src/tools/telegramCursorOperatorPolicy.ts) only clears a
  #      policy flag — it stops nothing.
  #   2. Building one is explicitly vetoed. `.swarmforge/operator/
  #      continuous-shifts.json` is armed with `scheduledStop: false` and the
  #      note "Do not restore night-stop / day-shift-end / cooldown 17:00-09:00
  #      until the human revokes" — first armed 2026-07-31, reaffirmed
  #      2026-08-03 and 2026-08-04. backlog/STEERING.md records the same
  #      directive as an explicit veto on this class of work.
  #
  # Unparking condition: the human revokes continuous-shifts (or asks for a
  # scheduled bedtime directly). At that point this becomes its own ticket —
  # the scheduler is a separate mechanism from the bedtime verb, which shipped
  # in BL-762 as an operator-invoked command and needs no change to be driven
  # by one.

  Background:
    Given a running swarm with its ancillary services up

  # BL-762 scheduled-end-of-day-picks-its-verb-06
  Scenario Outline: the scheduled end of day picks its verb from configuration
    Given the scheduled day-shift-end is configured <configuration>
    When the scheduled end of day runs
    Then <expected> is the verb invoked

    Examples:
      | configuration               | expected           |
      | with no lights-out override | the bedtime verb   |
      | for lights-out              | the full stop verb |
